.TH GPU 3
.SH NAME
gpu \- VirtIO GPU display and VirGL command transport
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind '#∇' /mnt/gpu

.BI /mnt/gpu/ clone
.BI /mnt/gpu/ caps
.BI /mnt/gpu/ stats
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /ctl
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /cmd
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /submit
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /wait
.BI /mnt/gpu/ n /resource
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The experimental
.I gpu
device provides off-screen VirGL rendering on the first VirtIO GPU PCI
device. It requires the modern VirtIO interface; rendering contexts
also require the VirGL feature.
It is included in the amd64 pc64 kernel.
The optional
.B virtio
display controller uses the same transport without changing the draw protocol.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
for reading and writing creates a context and returns its
.B ctl
file.
Only the host owner may open context files.
Reading
.B ctl
returns three decimal numbers: directory number, host context identifier,
and last allocated resource identifier (initially zero).
Context and resource identifiers are allocated by the kernel and are not reused.
Closing the last open file in a context releases its resources and the context.
.PP
.B caps
contains the binary little-endian VirGL capability set 1, version 1.
It is empty on devices without VirGL, which cannot open rendering contexts.
Applications must consult its format and feature masks before using
optional rendering features.
.PP
Each write to
.B cmd
submits one complete VirGL command stream of little-endian 32-bit words.
It must be nonempty, a multiple of four bytes, and no larger than 256 KiB.
The kernel supplies the context identifier and waits for a GPU fence
before returning.
Shader translation and VirGL command validation are performed by the host.
.PP
.B submit
accepts the same stream but returns after copying it into kernel memory and
notifying the device. Reading
.B wait
waits for submitted work and returns this context's latest submitted fence
as a decimal number, or zero before the first submission.
Use a read at offset zero for each wait.
There is one outstanding request for the device. The next submission,
control operation, backing access or context close completes it first.
Waiting may therefore also wait for another context's work.
Asynchronous errors are retained by the owning context and reported by
subsequent writes, backing reads and waits; closing still releases resources.
.PP
The following text commands are accepted by
.BR ctl .
Numbers may be decimal, octal, or hexadecimal.
.TP
.BI resource " target format bind width height depth array levels samples flags bytes"
Create a VirGL resource, allocate zeroed guest backing memory, and attach
it to the context.
The fields correspond to VirtIO RESOURCE_CREATE_3D;
.I levels
is the highest mip level, so zero means one level.
.I bytes
is the size of its guest backing store.
The new resource identifier appears in
.BR ctl .
A binary file with that decimal identifier appears in the context directory.
Reads and writes of this file access guest backing memory only;
they do not implicitly transfer data to or from the GPU.
.TP
.BI attach " context id"
Attach an existing resource to this context.
.I context
is the source's host context identifier, as returned by its
.B ctl
file; it is distinct from the directory number.
The resource must still be attached to that context.
The new attachment shares both GPU storage and guest backing memory,
and counts against this context's resource and memory limits.
A resource remains allocated until its last attachment is released.
Attaching a resource already present in this context is an error.
.TP
.BI upload " id level x y z width height depth offset stride layerstride"
Transfer a box from guest backing to the GPU resource.
.I offset
is a byte offset in the backing store.
Zero strides request the host's tightly packed layout.
.TP
.BI download " id level x y z width height depth offset stride layerstride"
Transfer a box from the GPU resource to guest backing.
The application can then read the resource file.
.TP
.BI unref " id"
Detach a resource from this context, freeing it on the last attachment.
Existing descriptors for that resource become invalid, even if its table
slot is later reused or the same resource is attached again.
.PP
Control operations, command submission and backing access are ordered.
All device requests are fenced.
A command timeout resets and disables the device before releasing DMA memory;
subsequent rendering requires rebooting the guest.
.SS Display
The
.B virtio
controller in
.IR vga (3)
presents the software screen through a separate 2D resource on scanout zero.
The initial screen must use
.B x8r8g8b8
with no rotation, at most 4096 by 4096 pixels.
Use the software cursor.
Screen damage is collected by a kernel process, which copies changed
rectangles into private backing memory and completes each transfer before
reusing it. The draw lock covers the copy, not GPU allocation or fence waits.
Screen updates are asynchronous and may be combined.
The framebuffer is recreated when the screen dimensions change.
VirGL is not required for this display path.
.PP
This replaces framebuffer writes to the emulated VGA device.
Drawing and window composition still use software; 3D clients still read
their rendered pixels back for libdraw.
Display errors are reported on the kernel console and stop updates until
the controller is selected again. Device failure still requires rebooting.
.SS Measurements
The read-only
.B stats
file contains cumulative decimal counters, one name and value per line:
.B requests
(completed device requests),
.B waitns
(time spent waiting for completion),
.B frames
(display copies),
.B bytes
(copied display bytes),
.B copyns
(pixel copying time), and
.B drawlockns
(time the display process holds the draw lock).
Times are nanoseconds. Differences between readings measure an interval;
wait time is not GPU execution time and excludes waiting to acquire locks.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
cd /sys/src/libgpu
mk install
cd /sys/src/cmd/gputest
mk
mk test
6.gputest
6.teapot
.EE
.PP
With a VirtIO VGA device and an existing 32-bit VESA software screen:
.EX
echo type virtio > /dev/vgactl
.EE
